payload.message:
Use the browser’s built-in Web Speech API. For a beta demo, that’s the fastest path.

Basic idea:
1. Ask for microphone access.
2. Start speech recognition.
3. Listen for transcript results.
4. Put the recognized text into React state.
5. Stop recognition when the turn ends.

Minimal example for React:

```jsx
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

export default function VoiceDemo() {
  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);
  const [listening, setListening] = useState(false);
  const [transcript, setTranscript] = useState("");
  const recognitionRef = useRef(null);

  function startListening() {
    const SpeechRecognition =
      window.SpeechRecognition || window.webkitSpeechRecognition;

    if (!SpeechRecognition) {
      setSupported(false);
      return;
    }

    const recognition = new SpeechRecognition();
    recognitionRef.current = recognition;

    recognition.continuous = true;
    recognition.interimResults = true;
    recognition.lang = "en-US";

    recognition.onstart = function () {
      setListening(true);
    };

    recognition.onresult = function (event) {
      let finalText = "";
      let interimText = "";

      for (let i = event.resultIndex; i < event.results.length; i += 1) {
        const text = event.results[i][0].transcript;

        if (event.results[i].isFinal) {
          finalText += text + " ";
        } else {
          interimText += text;
        }
      }

      setTranscript((prev) => prev + finalText + interimText);
    };

    recognition.onend = function () {
      setListening(false);
    };

    recognition.onerror = function (event) {
      console.error("Speech recognition error:", event.error);
      setListening(false);
    };

    recognition.start();
  }

  function stopListening() {
    if (recognitionRef.current) {
      recognitionRef.current.stop();
    }
  }

  if (!supported) {
    return <p>Your browser does not support speech recognition.</p>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={startListening} disabled={listening}>
        Start Recording
      </button>

      <button onClick={stopListening} disabled={!listening}>
        Stop Recording
      </button>

      <p>{listening ? "Listening..." : "Not listening"}</p>
      <textarea value={transcript} readOnly rows={10} cols={50} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

A couple important beta notes:
- This works best in Chrome.
- `webkitSpeechRecognition` is the version you’ll likely use.
- The browser will prompt for microphone permission automatically.
- Don’t worry about audio files for now; just capture text.

One bug to avoid:
In `onresult`, don’t keep appending both final and interim text permanently into the same string every event, or you’ll get duplicated text. A cleaner beta pattern is:
- keep `finalTranscript` in one state
- keep `interimTranscript` in another
- render `finalTranscript + interimTranscript`

Cleaner version of the state idea:

```jsx
const [finalTranscript, setFinalTranscript] = useState("");
const [interimTranscript, setInterimTranscript] = useState("");
```

Then in `onresult`:

```jsx
recognition.onresult = function (event) {
  let newFinal = "";
  let newInterim = "";

  for (let i = event.resultIndex; i < event.results.length; i += 1) {
    const text = event.results[i][0].transcript;

    if (event.results[i].isFinal) {
      newFinal += text + " ";
    } else {
      newInterim += text;
    }
  }

  if (newFinal) {
    setFinalTranscript((prev) => prev + newFinal);
  }

  setInterimTranscript(newInterim);
};
```

And when stopping a turn:
- save `finalTranscript.trim()` as that player’s turn
- clear `finalTranscript` and `interimTranscript`
- move to next player or enable `End Debate`

So the simplest flow for your beta is:
- `Start Recording`
- user speaks
- transcript appears live
- `Stop Recording`
- save that text as one turn
- after 8 turns, send all saved turns to AI

If you want, I can next give you a CourtClash-specific React component for `Player A / Player B turns + live transcript + End Debate`.

